### 2. Under the Hood

When the Zig compiler encounters a `comptime` expression, it doesn't emit code that runs later—it immediately evaluates the expression using an internal interpreter that executes ZIR (Zig Intermediate Representation). This interpreter has access to the full language, including function calls, allocations, and even I/O (though I/O at compile time is strongly discouraged). The result of evaluation is a compile-time–known value that gets embedded directly into the final binary as a constant or immediate operand.

**Constant propagation.** After comptime evaluation, the produced constants participate in the usual optimisation pipeline. LLVM (or the self-hosted x86_64 backend) sees them as `constant` values in its IR, enabling constant folding, dead code elimination, and instruction selection that exploits known immediates. For example, a comptime-computed array used in a switch or lookup becomes a base address plus an offset; if the index is also comptime-known, the whole memory load can be replaced by the exact value.

**Compile-time memory model.** Zig's comptime interpreter manages its own heap. Allocations made via an `Allocator` (even the `std.heap.page_allocator`) during comptime are tracked and freed when the compilation unit finishes—you don't pay for them at runtime. This allows building complex data structures (hash maps, trees) entirely at compile time and then embedding their final flattened form. However, note that the allocator used must be available in the comptime environment; `std.heap.page_allocator` works, but a custom allocator that relies on runtime operating system calls will fail to compile.

**Function specialisation.** When a function takes a `comptime` parameter, Zig effectively generates a separate monomorphised copy for each distinct compile‑time value. The body of the function is re‑evaluated during semantic analysis with that parameter bound, allowing the compiler to perform aggressive optimisations tailored to that value. The resulting machine code sees the `comptime` parameter as a literal constant. This is similar to C++ template instantiation, but with the key difference that the body is interpreted as normal Zig code with full comptime capabilities, not an AST-level substitution.

**Limits and quotas.** The interpreter imposes a branch evaluation quota (default 1000 backwards branches per comptime invocation) to prevent infinite loops from hanging the compiler. You can increase it with `@setEvalBranchQuota` if your comptime logic genuinely needs more iterations—common when generating large lookup tables or unrolling loops with high trip counts.

