### 8. Examples

The following examples are complete, compilable, and designed to illustrate real‑world usage.

#### Example 1: Compile‑Time CRC‑8 Table

```zig
const std = @import("std");

/// Polynomial for CRC‑8‑ATM (x^8 + x^2 + x + 1)
const CRC8_POLY: u8 = 0x07;

/// Generate the 256‑byte lookup table at compile time.
const crc8_table: [256]u8 = blk: {
    var table: [256]u8 = undefined;
    for (0..256) |i| {
        var crc: u8 = @intCast(i);
        for (0..8) |_| {
            if (crc & 0x80 != 0) {
                crc = (crc << 1) ^ CRC8_POLY;
            } else {
                crc <<= 1;
            }
        }
        table[i] = crc;
    }
    break :blk table;
};

/// Compute CRC‑8 of a byte slice using the comptime table.
pub fn crc8(data: []const u8) u8 {
    var crc: u8 = 0;
    for (data) |byte| {
        crc = crc8_table[crc ^ byte];
    }
    return crc;
}

test "CRC8 known answer" {
    const input = [_]u8{ 0x31, 0x32, 0x33, 0x34 }; // "1234"
    const result = crc8(&input);
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(u8, 0xA0), result);
}
```

The table lives in the `.rodata` section. The runtime `crc8` function is a trivial loop with a single table lookup per byte—no initialisation overhead.

#### Example 2: Compile‑Time Unrolled Dot Product for Fixed‑Size Vectors

```zig
/// Compute dot product of two vectors of length N, unrolled at compile time.
pub fn dot(comptime N: usize, a: @Vector(N, f32), b: @Vector(N, f32)) f32 {
    // The @reduce builtin will be lowered to efficient SIMD shuffle + add.
    const mul = a * b;
    return @reduce(.Add, mul);
}
```

For `N` known at compile time, the compiler generates an optimal sequence; for small `N` it may be fully unrolled scalar operations. The `@Vector` type naturally works with comptime lengths.

#### Example 3: Compile‑Time Struct Validator & Serialiser Generator

This example demonstrates how to use `@typeInfo` to generate a serialiser that avoids runtime reflection and allocates no memory.

```zig
const std = @import("std");

/// Validate at compile time that every field of a struct is a scalar integer.
fn assertAllInts(comptime T: type) void {
    const info = @typeInfo(T);
    if (info != .Struct) @compileError("Expected a struct, got " ++ @typeName(T));
    for (info.Struct.fields) |field| {
        const field_info = @typeInfo(field.type);
        if (field_info != .Int) {
            @compileError("Field " ++ field.name ++ " must be an integer, got " ++ @typeName(field.type));
        }
    }
}

/// Generate a serialise function that writes the struct's fields as little‑endian bytes.
pub fn Serialiser(comptime T: type) type {
    assertAllInts(T);
    return struct {
        /// Write the fields of a value into the given writer.
        pub fn write(value: T, writer: anytype) !void {
            inline for (std.meta.fields(T)) |field| {
                const bytes = std.mem.asBytes(&@field(value, field.name));
                try writer.writeAll(bytes);
            }
        }
    };
}
```

Usage:

```zig
const MyConfig = struct {
    baud: u32,
    flags: u16,
};

const serialiser = Serialiser(MyConfig);
// In some runtime code:
// try serialiser.write(config, &writer);
```

The `inline for` over `std.meta.fields` unrolls at compile time into a sequence of `writer.writeAll` calls, one per field. No loop, no reflection, no dynamic dispatch.

